---
// Custom Audio Atlas case study. Replaces the Notion render. Notion still
// drives metadata (title, tags, cover, order) for the homepage list;
// only the body content is custom.
//
// Narrative spine: I built the audio catalog I'd always wanted, and the
// row inside it grew into a workbench. Each section is a moment of use,
// not a moment of code. The interactives carry the story.

import CSLayout from "../../components/case-study/CSLayout.astro";
import CSHero from "../../components/case-study/CSHero.astro";
import CSSection from "../../components/case-study/CSSection.astro";
import CSPullquote from "../../components/case-study/CSPullquote.astro";
import CSStatGrid from "../../components/case-study/CSStatGrid.astro";
import CSLiveDemo from "../../components/case-study/CSLiveDemo.astro";
import BucketCardShowcase from "../../components/case-study/showcase/BucketCardShowcase.astro";
import CardEvolutionScrubber from "../../components/case-study/showcase/CardEvolutionScrubber.astro";
import PromoteButtonDemo from "../../components/case-study/showcase/PromoteButtonDemo.astro";
import VerbsHub from "../../components/case-study/showcase/VerbsHub.astro";
import EngineerPairCard from "../../components/case-study/showcase/EngineerPairCard.astro";
import FilterChipStrip from "../../components/case-study/showcase/FilterChipStrip.astro";
import LoopCropDemo from "../../components/case-study/showcase/LoopCropDemo.astro";
import TranscriptShowcase from "../../components/case-study/showcase/TranscriptShowcase.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<CSLayout
  title="Audio Atlas — Jon Tollefson"
  description="The catalog I built for the way I actually use audio. The row in it grew into a workbench. Live, interactive case study."
  ogImage="/case-studies/the-console/cover.jpg"
>
  <CSHero
    eyebrow="Case study 02 · May 2026 — present"
    title="Audio Atlas"
    subtitle="The catalog I built for the way I actually use audio."
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Designer, builder, sole engineer" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "May 2026 — present" },
      { label: "Stack", value: "Astro · FastAPI · Python · Pedalboard · Claude" },
      { label: "Status", value: "Shipped · Engineer live 2026-05-22" },
    ]}
  />

  {/* ─────── 01 · THE PITCH ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="01" title="The pitch" deck="Sit down to make something. The blank Ableton session waits. You half-remember a vocal you recorded on your phone last Tuesday. You open Finder. iCloud hasn't downloaded the file yet. By the time it shows up the idea is gone." noTopRule>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>That moment, repeated a thousand times across eight years of writing music, is the gap this catalog closes. Twenty thousand audio files (every voice memo, every jam, every bounce, every sample I've ever touched) sit in one place now. Every row plays the instant you click its waveform. You can crop a four-bar slice out of a thirty-minute jam in ten seconds. You can peel a vocal off its instrumental with one click. You can promote a loop straight into Ableton Live's browser as a normalized, named, ready-to-drag file. You can hand a finished track to a mix agent and get six taste variants back to A/B between.</p>

      <p>None of that existed in the first version of this site. Three weeks ago this was a default-Squarespace-shaped portfolio that couldn't play a single audio file. What follows is the journey of one row in a catalog learning what it could do, told through real iterations and live components you can touch.</p>

      <CSPullquote hero>
        The page count went down as the card got more powerful. Five portal tabs collapsed into three. Each row now does the work the old tabs were supposed to.
      </CSPullquote>
    </div>

    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 20279, label: "files in the catalog", color: "white" },
        { n: 14, label: "verbs per row (was 2)", color: "yellow" },
        { n: 3, label: "portal tabs (was 5+)", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 6, label: "mix variants per job", color: "pink" },
      ]}
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 02 · THE CARD GREW UP ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="02" title="The card grew up" deck="Seven eras. Each one is a real git commit where the row earned a new verb. Drag the slider — watch the row's capability light up underneath as it learns what it can do.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · DRAG TO SCRUB"
      caption="One markup, seven design eras, swapped via a single class. The chip strip is the actual capability arc of the production card across three weeks. Watch E5 morph into a crop surface. Watch E6 open a pipeline beneath itself."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <CardEvolutionScrubber />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Before this, the row was a filename in Finder. You couldn't hear it. You couldn't see what was in it. You couldn't do anything to it without leaving the catalog and opening another app. Each era below earned the row one new thing it could do for itself.</p>

      <p>The pattern that emerged: every time the row could do more by itself, a whole UI surface could be deleted. The dedicated Listen page went away once the waveform played. The Library tab went away once Buckets could filter and sort. Workbench went away once cropping and stem-splitting moved into the card's expand panel. The portal got smaller and more powerful in the same week.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 03 · TOUCH IT ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="03" title="Touch the current state" deck="The production BucketCard with the full action surface. Click the waveform to hear it loop (real audio). Click ✂ Crop to slice. Click 2-stem or 4-stem to split. Click Send to Engineer to queue a mix job. Nothing actually deletes, splits, or sends — but every affordance is wired.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · TRY EVERY VERB"
      caption="The actual production markup. The waveform plays a procedurally-synthesised drum loop at the card's own BPM; ✂ Crop opens an inline crop frame on the waveform; 2/4-stem flips through a fake demucs render and reveals the resulting child rows; Send to Engineer queues a fake job for six A/B variants. Three variants below show the same component across other catalog states."
      bleed
    >
      <BucketCardShowcase
        title="NOIZU_125_shaker_loop_tight_01"
        ext=".wav"
        duration="0:04"
        bpm={99}
        keyStr="E major"
        date={{ rel: "1y", stamp: "06-15-2024", days: "704d" }}
        bucket="loops"
        tags={["loop", "sample", "drum"]}
        contentType="music-only"
        sourceType="splice"
        expanded
        interactive
        hideStems={false}
      />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="card-grid-2">
      <BucketCardShowcase
        cardId="bc-voice"
        title="VOX_Tolo_0007"
        ext=".wav"
        duration="0:30"
        date={{ rel: "1y", stamp: "10-23-2024", days: "574d" }}
        silenceRatio={0.42}
        bucket="field"
        contentType="voice-note"
        sourceType="voice_memo"
        tags={["idea-fragment", "first-person"]}
      />
      <BucketCardShowcase
        cardId="bc-inlive"
        title="55_Cmaj_0_what_you_know"
        ext=".wav"
        duration="0:08"
        bpm={55}
        keyStr="C major"
        date={{ rel: "5d", stamp: "05-17-2026", days: "5d" }}
        bucket="loops"
        inLive
        contentType="loop"
        tags={["loop", "sample"]}
        expanded
      />
    </div>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>This is the row that used to be a filename. The audio plays inside it. The metadata edits inside it. The tags you used to manage in seven different surfaces live in the expand panel. The row stopped being a label on a file and became the place where you work with the file.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 04 · THE FILTER GREW UP TOO ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="04" title="The filter grew up too" deck="Your sample library is twelve thousand files across forty-seven sub-folders. You know there's a perfect snare somewhere in there. You give up and use a stock one. That's the problem this strip is for.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · CLICK A TAG"
      caption="The catalog organizes itself in front of you. Click 'songwriting' — the strip re-ranks, the row count drops, the strip shows you what tags co-occur with what you picked. Add 'first-person' to compound it. The view becomes the index."
      bleed
    >
      <FilterChipStrip />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The chips are three kinds at once. Engine-derived classifications (content_type, source_type), curator-added tags from the expand panel of each card, and the frequency-ranked top-ten of whatever's in the current view. Click any of them and the row count narrows. Counts beside each chip update to reflect what's actually left. The system never asks you to read a documentation page about how filtering works — the chips show what's there and what filtering by it would do.</p>
      <p>There's no auto-suggested taxonomy. No vibe-tags I didn't write. The tags you see came from voice-memo transcripts I let Whisper read and Claude classify, plus the tags I typed into card expand panels. The catalog is a mirror of my own listening, not a model's guess at it.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 05 · THE FILES START SPEAKING ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="05" title="The files start speaking" deck="You record ninety minutes of jamming. The file lands on your desktop as Audio 14.wav. A week later you can't tell which of the seventeen Audio NN.wav files on your desktop has the part you actually liked. Whisper transcribes. Claude summarizes. Now the row tells you what's in it.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · 3 ROWS, 3 SUMMARIES"
      caption="Three real catalog rows, three real-vibe Claude outputs. The point isn't classification — it's pulling humanity back out of the silos. Multi-hour rambling voice memos become one-sentence character studies. A jam session becomes a logline. The files keep all their audio, but they also keep words about themselves now."
      bleed
    >
      <TranscriptShowcase />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>This is the part of the build I didn't know I needed until it started returning summaries. Eight years of audio I'd never gone back to becomes eight years of rows I'd actually read. A jam session I forgot existed surfaces because Claude noticed two 16-bar sections worth pulling. A voice memo gets flagged because the same conspiratorial chorus theory has shown up six times across the archive. The engine isn't just classifying. It's surfacing patterns of a person across years of material.</p>
      <p>The bigger thesis underneath: intense data sweeping is the way you extract understanding and humanity out of silo'd static files. Whisper, Claude, Librosa each do one narrow thing on every row at ingest. None of them are creative. Together they let the catalog be a mirror of how I've been listening, not a model's guess at it.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 06 · RIP A LOOP FROM A JAM ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="06" title="Rip a loop out of a thirty-minute jam" deck="Pull a 12-bar chunk out of a 5:42 jam in ten seconds. Drag the cyan frame to slide. Drag a handle to retrim. Spacebar plays only what's between the caps.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Before this existed, the same task meant opening the jam in Ableton, eyeballing the warp markers, dragging a loop region, bouncing the selection, renaming the bounce, dragging the new file into Live's browser. Five minutes of context-switching per loop, every loop, forever. Most loops never got pulled out at all because the cost of pulling one out was so high.</p>
    </div>

    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · DRAG · ⌘ SNAP · SPACE TO LOOP"
      caption="Bar-snap on means the caps grab the nearest bar boundary (so the loop will actually loop). Bar-snap off lets you trim by milliseconds. Export drops a normalized .wav into _Soundbending/loops/extracted/."
      bleed
    >
      <LoopCropDemo />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Now it lives inside the catalog row, where the audio already is. The crop tool only matters because the catalog already knew the tempo. Librosa runs on every audio row at ingest. When BPM and key are populated, every loop tool downstream gets to assume them. The cropper draws bar ticks. The promoter writes BPM and key into the filename. The mix engineer's stem renderer crossfades at section boundaries. One analysis pass at the catalog level lights up every tool downstream.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 07 · THE BRIDGE TO ABLETON ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="07" title="The card writes into Ableton" deck="You finish a rough mix. You bounce it. You name it v1. Two days later you bounce a different version, also v1. Which one had the kick where you wanted it? Before the Promote button, every loop and bounce I made was the start of an organizational mistake.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The Promote button settles all of that. One click writes the loop into Live's own browser, normalized to -14 LUFS, named with its BPM and key, ready to drag onto a clip slot. The instinct when extending a DAW is to build a new surface inside it. The right move was the opposite. Ableton already drags audio onto clip slots from any folder you register as a "Place," so the engine just writes into a Place I registered once. The DAW's own gesture does the work. No plugin, no Max device, no custom audio API.</p>
    </div>

    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · CLICK PROMOTE"
      caption="The button is real. The pipeline is real. The animation timing approximates the actual sub-second-per-loop behavior of the M1 (loudnorm is the slowest of the three steps). Re-running is idempotent; the file overwrites in place."
    >
      <PromoteButtonDemo />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Earlier in the build I tried the opposite approach, a Max-for-Live device that rendered the catalog inside Ableton itself. Two architectural walls killed it. The pivot to writing into the affordance instead of building inside it became the spine of the whole build, not just this feature.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 08 · THE MIX ENGINEER ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="08" title="An engineer that learns my taste" deck="You finish a track. You want to A/B against a reference, a Tame Impala cut maybe. You jump out of Ableton, open Spotify, miss the timing, forget what you were comparing, give up. A/B is the most honest mixing tool there is and it's the one DAWs make hardest.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The mix engineer is the second pipeline downstream of the row. Where Promote-to-Live ships a clean loop into the DAW, the Engineer ships six full-mix takes back into the catalog for me to judge. The three axes:</p>
      <ul class="cs-axes">
        <li><strong class="ax-cyan">focal</strong> · forward_bright vs recessed_warm</li>
        <li><strong class="ax-pink">space</strong> · punchy_dry vs spacious_wet</li>
        <li><strong class="ax-yellow">dynamics</strong> · modern_compressed vs vintage_open</li>
      </ul>
      <p>One pair card at a time. A on the left, B on the right. Click to switch playback sides. Free-text comment per pair persists into the system. The next render on the same source reads the full comment history. A space-axis retry mentioning a buried kick will also address a vocal complaint from an earlier focal pair on the same track. The system learns my taste one pair at a time.</p>
      <p>After each variant render, five simulated playback chains report what the mix will do on phone, AirPods, laptop, car, and club systems. Per-band energy deltas translate to plain-language flags: "phone: vocal disappears", "club: stereo collapses", "translates clean". Mix decisions get made against actual listening contexts, not against the abstract idea of a balanced mix.</p>
    </div>

    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · TINDER A vs B"
      caption="The production pair card. Click A/B (or ←/→), pick a side, leave a comment. Spacebar plays a faux loop with a moving playhead. Stem solo buttons swap which stem plays on both sides simultaneously, so the comparison stays apples-to-apples."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <EngineerPairCard />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The reference catalog shipped on 2026-05-22. Bon Iver, Tame Impala, Frank Ocean, others I love, each row carrying per-track notes on what works in it. The planner pulls the closest references for the source into its prompt context as taste anchors before any variant gets rendered. The mixes come back already shaped by what I've told the engine matters. Closest-reference is text-matched today; audio-feature similarity is the next pass.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 09 · UNDER THE HOOD ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="09" title="The verbs underneath" deck="Every interaction in the card is one of these endpoints. Tap any to see the actual shape. The catalog is the contract — surfaces above it are disposable.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · CLICK A VERB"
      caption="When the Surprise and Library tabs got deleted on 2026-05-17, the catalog didn't change. Three surfaces collapsed into one. That's what verbs-as-contract buys: I can throw out the UI without throwing out the data."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <VerbsHub />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <CSPullquote cite="from the session log that triggered the 3-tab consolidation, 2026-05-17">
      "i think at this point it'd make sense to consolidate the console's ui. library doesn't make sense anymore as a separate page, the bucket's page is really the library now... 'surprise' page also feels like bloat now."
    </CSPullquote>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 10 · THE BOUNDARY ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="10" title="What it deliberately does not do" deck="A music tool I trust is a music tool that doesn't pretend to author. This list is short on purpose and gets checked before any feature lands.">
    <ul class="wont">
      <li>Write Substack, LinkedIn, or Instagram posts without an explicit draft invocation.</li>
      <li>Propose albums, EPs, or song concepts.</li>
      <li>Push daily prompts.</li>
      <li>Listen to voice commands during a session.</li>
      <li>Touch the desktop.</li>
      <li>Publish to streaming platforms.</li>
      <li>Make creative decisions on my behalf.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Every one of those is a feature an adjacent product ships. Every one is a place where the machine would pretend to author. A catalog that emails me an unsolicited "draft album concept" stops being a catalog and becomes a parasocial collaborator. That trade was off the table from the start.</p>
      <p>Whisper transcribes. Claude classifies. The Engineer renders variants and reasons about them. None of them synthesize. The synthesis happens later, in Live, by hand.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 11 · CLOSING ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="11" title="Where this points" deck="The point of the build is not the build. The point is more music made.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Adjacent to all of this is a Diary tab that pairs random voice-memo fragments with random catalog audio, so a melody you hummed into your phone in 2023 can surface next to an instrumental sketch you made last week. Coming back to a project six months later, trying to remember what the idea was and where the pieces went, is the friction the catalog was originally built to remove. The Diary is the surface where that friction has nothing left to hide behind.</p>
      <p><code>uxjon.com/sounds</code> already exists as a public route. Items flip to public via a toggle on any card. The infrastructure for the future state (a public-facing surface of finished work, streaming from the same engine that catalogs the raw material) is already shipped. The path from "this loop is interesting" to "this track is out" runs through the same database and the same verbs.</p>
      <p>3,750 rows still sit in the inbox bucket. 215 promotable loops are queued. The machine just made choosing cheaper.</p>
    </div>

    <CSPullquote>
      Utility, not synthesis. The computer sorts, fingerprints, surfaces, and removes format friction. The artist still chooses, still drags, still arranges.
    </CSPullquote>
  </CSSection>

  <div class="cs-foot">
    <a href="/#work" class="cs-foot-back">← back to selected work</a>
  </div>
</CSLayout>

<style>
  .prose-block { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block > .cs-pullquote { max-width: 64ch; }

  .card-grid-2 {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 18px;
    margin: 24px 0 8px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 880px) {
    .card-grid-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }

  .cs-axes {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 18px 0 22px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
    gap: 8px 22px;
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .cs-axes li {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.85);
    padding: 8px 12px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.10);
    border-radius: 3px;
    background: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.02);
  }
  .cs-axes .ax-cyan { color: var(--brand-cyan); font-style: italic; }
  .cs-axes .ax-pink { color: var(--brand-pink); font-style: italic; }
  .cs-axes .ax-yellow { color: var(--brand-yellow); font-style: italic; }

  .wont {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 18px 0 28px;
    max-width: 64ch;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 6px 22px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .wont { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  .wont li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 22px;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.78);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wont li::before {
    content: "—";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--brand-pink);
  }

  .cs-foot {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 64px 0 0;
  }
  .cs-foot-back {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.32);
    border-radius: 2px;
    transition: color 160ms ease, border-color 160ms ease, background 160ms ease;
  }
  .cs-foot-back:hover {
    color: #f5f1e8;
    border-color: var(--brand-orange);
    background: rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.08);
  }
</style>
